# [[ .Name ]]

A [tug](https://github.com/cuonggt/tug) app: Go handlers render [[ .Framework ]] pages
through Inertia, with no API in between.

```sh
tug dev      # run it on http://localhost:8080, rebuilt and reloaded as it changes
tug gen      # write the TypeScript of the pages' props and the routes
tug build    # build it into one binary, frontend included
go test ./...
```

- `main.go`: the server, its pages and routes. A page is declared with
  `tug.Page[Props]("Name")`, and its component is
  `resources/js/pages/Name.[[ .Component ]]`. Its pages run the scripts
  that carry their nonce alone, by a Content-Security-Policy, as
  `app.html`'s do: what it blocks, the log says.
- `resources/js/app.[[ .Script ]]`: the app in the browser, which `inertia.[[ .Script ]]`
[[- if .SSR ]]
  makes, and `ssr.[[ .Script ]]`, the app on the server. A first visit's page
  renders there first, with Node, which the app runs beside it from the
  SSR build in `ssr/build`; under tug dev, Vite renders it.
[[- else ]]
  makes.
[[- end ]]
- `lang/`: the app's languages, a file each, as `tug lang vi` writes
  `lang/vi.json` with every text the app says, to translate. The binary
  embeds them, and `APP_LOCALE` is the one a browser gets that asks for
  none of them.
- `resources/js/tug`: the TypeScript tug gen writes from the Go types. It
  isn't edited by hand; tug dev and tug build write it again.
- `.env`: read by tug dev. `APP_KEY` encrypts the sessions, and a deployed
  app needs one of its own, set in its environment, with `APP_URL`, its
  own address, and `TRUSTED_PROXIES` behind a load balancer.
[[ if .SSR -]]
- `Dockerfile`: the app as one static binary on a distroless image with
  Node, for the pages it renders on the server.
[[- else -]]
- `Dockerfile`: the app as one static binary on a distroless image.
[[- end ]]
